e actors  here especially in the final act where  we're with this core four it's just  outstanding  i'm wondering though what is that  advantage you have as having so much  acting experience before directing what  what could you kind of learn from acting  that you kind of brought to your  directing i think the main thing to me  is that i know what it is to be in that  position and it of course it looks like  this really joyful thing to do and and  something that you know it almost looks  like actors want that attention and they  choose to be in the middle of it all but  actors have so much shame so i think if  you're capable as a director to make  them feel safe and also to sort of guide  them and to tell them because these were  the things that were told to me when i  was very young and i really enjoyed that  people say like the more you prepare the  better you know your lines the more  you're able to let go in the moments you  know so and often especially movie  actors or film actors can can have the  misunderstanding that is better to keep  everything fresh and for some films that  might work you know and to not learn  your lines so well and to keep but i  think it's really great if you learn to  sort of especially if you have a writer  like sarah the lab who creates music  with her language that you are really  really really thoroughly prepared and  then when you actually do it you don't  you don't panic because you think oh my  god i'm losing my lines or oh my god i  don't know where what my blocking is i  don't know where to go so the better you  rehearse and then you let go in a moment  and then you can even create space for  them to improvise or to have their own  ideas because you're so well prepared as  a director and an actor then you can  both let go and really dance you know  that tango so that's yeah  yeah and i like you mentioned the  improvising i was going to ask you about  that especially when you have someone  like a pete davidson on set was there a  lot of room in these scenes where  we didn't see even different takes you  guys had of different scenes like was  there a lot of improvising going on in  general yeah of course i think i had  very specific spaces in mind even before  we started where i was like okay here  i'm going to ask him or her  to do you know a bit of improv um so we  would always do the scene completely as  written on the page and we would you  know cover all of that and shoot all  that and then we would always do takes  in which certain people got like that  those liberties at certain specific  times so that they wouldn't you know be  in each other's ways but really could do  that and yes he brought a lot to the  table um that wasn't in the script but  also rachel senate and also amandla  stemberg like a man left for instance at  that moment where she talks about  ableist you know that  she came up with that um and so and some  of them also had ideas like you know we  would come on set with breakfast and  they would come up to me and say like  hey wouldn't it be more fun if i said  this or isn't it funnier if i do that  and um you know a lot of it was it was  actually improvised or came from them  during the table read or rehearsals  yeah and you could tell and there's like  a natural rhythm to the film where it  just feels real too so it really  immerses you in that sense as an  audience member which i love and i'm  also wondering too because you're  filming mostly in same locations did  that give you  the  easier way to do things where you filmed  chronological order most of this film or  did you kind of  film  for it gave us a lot of freedom in the  sense that we had more time because of  course if you would have to move between  you know i don't know if you have to  drive for miles to another house or  whatever so we really restricted  ourselves to really say before we found  that house like it needs to be one house  we need to find a house that is so big  and so decadent that it actually has all  those different rooms that sarah and i  created on the page  but once we were there um it was we  didn't we weren't able to shoot in the  chronological order because it just  didn't work as far as the lighting  because as you could see  so much of it is in the dark so you had  to be super creative with light because  me being an actor i've been on movie  sets where i only shot nights you know  where you don't sleep anymore and i just  didn't want that for them so i was like  we need to find tricks so that we can do  half a day half a night and so we didn't  shoot in chronological order but it was  absolutely wonderful to be all together  in that house it was covered they were  that was not wonderful but the fact that  we were all imprisoned and that we were  so close to the house in our little  hotel  i think that really helped us to create  that friendship but also to create the  feeling of being in the pressure cooker  you know that basically the crew and i  and all the actors were when we were  shooting  yeah and i i like you mentioned the  lights because i was asking about that  because of a lot of it being in the dark  you had to get creative with the  lighting and this so was it a use of a  lot of natural lighting that's even in  the scene or were there also tricks you  were using with like artificial lighting  that we weren't seeing we did use some  tricks but my dp os provolf he also  comes from the netherlands we we shot  instinct together he's a genius but he's  he's a very hardcore european which  means that he doesn't really believe in  all the artificial things so of course  we add little things but he would really  take pride in the fact that they would  actually have to light each other and  that it was you know we had moments  because the house was literally like a  crazy labyrinth so we would actually  like make it pitch black dark you know  and it was just crazy if you would go to  the toilet you'd be oh my god where am i  gonna end 